Ohle Claussen!

> I confess that I never looked into the topic of computationally solving
> Sudoku, but certainly that depends on the numbers given at start?

The general approach to solving finite NP-complete problems like Sudoku is
backtracking, which only in run time depends on the numbers given at
start. Current PC hardware can solve typical Sudoku puzzles by
backtracking in few seconds at most.

> Most of the puzzles published in newspapers etc. can be easily solved by
> brute force, and IMO the scaling doesn't matter much when there's just a
> finite (and quite low) number of possibilities. Sokoban solvers, on the
> other hand, tend to do a lot of guesswork and run for a very long time,
> as they (have to?) use backtracking.

Sokoban is NP-hard and PSPACE-complete (proofs exist). It is not
necessarily finite and can not be solved by generalized backtracking.
Except you can try and hope to find a solution, of course. Sokoban solvers
might employ depth-first search to a limited depth, increasing the limit
if it didn't work, then trying again.

Humans typically solve Sokoban heuristically, while Sudoku can be
partially solved algorithmically, usually only leaving very few fields to
brute-force.


